  "efficiency_vs_completeness": {
    "_the_residual_is_not_efficiency": "Arms A and B are the SAME function one argument apart, emitting an identical record per node, so an encoding advantage is impossible by construction. The residual ~1.2x is average PATH LENGTH: arm A only reaches shallow nodes, whose paths are shorter. Measured on the L5 payloads, non-path characters per node are 146.5 (A) vs 140.0 (B) - arm A is marginally WORSE per node. Read the honest conclusion as: essentially ALL of the raw advantage is reduced coverage, not tighter encoding.",
    "_this_comparison_is_an_ablation_not_a_competitor": "B_inspect_scene_deep is SYNAPSE calling its own function with a bigger depth. It is the fairest ABLATION (nothing differs but how much scene is sent) and it is NOT an outside-in tool. It therefore cannot, on its own, support any claim of the form 'cheaper than sending the scene'.",
    "_what": "raw_cost_advantage_x is how much cheaper arm A LOOKS. true_efficiency_advantage_x is how much cheaper it is per node actually delivered. The gap between the two columns is the part of the headline that is 'sent less of the scene' rather than 'encoded it better'.",
